WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.328
Ollama and LM Studio: packaged local inference.

2
00:00:03.608 --> 00:00:08.301
Ollama packages model download, storage, and serving behind one command.

3
00:00:08.581 --> 00:00:10.096
ollama pull llama3.

4
00:00:10.376 --> 00:00:14.856
2:3b fetches a quantized model from its library (the Llama 3.

5
00:00:15.136 --> 00:00:23.008
2 3B tag is about 2 GB), and the background server listens on localhost:11434.

6
00:00:23.288 --> 00:00:42.680
Its native API has /api/chat and /api/generate endpoints that stream newline-delimited JSON by default; the last chunk, marked done: true, carries the timing fields that the lab uses: load_duration, prompt_eval_count and prompt_eval_duration (prefill),

7
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:49.232
eval_count and eval_duration (decode), and total_duration, all durations in nanoseconds.

8
00:00:49.512 --> 00:00:57.789
The API reference gives the decode speed formula directly: eval_count divided by eval_duration, times 10 to the 9th.

9
00:00:58.069 --> 00:01:12.789
Ollama also accepts a JSON schema in the format field for structured output, keeps a model loaded for five minutes after use by default ( keep_alive ), and exposes an OpenAI-compatible API under /v1.

10
00:01:13.069 --> 00:01:19.832
LM Studio is a desktop app for finding, downloading, and chatting with models, with a local server for code.

11
00:01:20.112 --> 00:01:21.797
Its docs say it runs llama.

12
00:01:22.077 --> 00:01:32.339
cpp (GGUF) models and, on Apple Silicon Macs, MLX models too, and it ships an lms command-line tool for loading models and starting the server.

13
00:01:32.619 --> 00:01:52.331
Its OpenAI-compatible endpoints include /v1/chat/completions, /v1/responses, /v1/embeddings, and /v1/models, and its examples use http://localhost:1234/v1.

14
00:01:52.611 --> 00:01:57.987
It is a good choice when you or a teammate want a GUI to compare models before writing code.

15
00:01:58.267 --> 00:02:00.869
Choosing between them is mostly about workflow.

16
00:02:01.149 --> 00:02:05.565
Ollama is convenient for scripts, servers, and teammates who live in a terminal.

17
00:02:05.845 --> 00:02:10.389
LM Studio suits interactive comparison and people who prefer a GUI.

18
00:02:10.669 --> 00:02:11.437
llama.

19
00:02:11.717 --> 00:02:16.005
cpp directly gives you every knob and the newest features first.

20
00:02:16.285 --> 00:02:21.192
All three serve GGUF models, so a quantized file you trust can move between them.
